        </div>

        <p>To accurately calculate your take-home income and personal tax bill, try our interactive <a href="/countries/singapore/income-tax/income-tax-calculator" class="text-link hover:underline">Singapore Income Tax Calculator</a>.</p>

        <h2 id="tax-residency">183-Day Tax Residency & Taxation for Foreigners</h2>
        <p>Understanding your tax residency status is crucial when living or working in Singapore. Under IRAS regulations, you are deemed a <strong>tax resident</strong> if you are:</p>
        <ol>
          <li>A Singapore Citizen or Permanent Resident (PR) who normally resides in Singapore; or</li>
          <li>A foreigner who has physically stayed or worked in Singapore for <strong>183 days or more</strong> within a calendar year.</li>
        </ol>

        <p>Tax residents enjoy progressive tax rates (0% to 24%) and are eligible to claim personal tax reliefs. In contrast, <strong>non-residents</strong> are taxed differently:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Employment Income:</strong> Taxed at a flat rate of 15% or the resident progressive rate, whichever results in a higher tax liability.</li>
          <li><strong>Director Fees & Consultancy Income:</strong> Taxed at a flat non-resident rate of 24%.</li>
          <li><strong>No Relief Eligibility:</strong> Non-residents cannot claim personal reliefs, Central Provident Fund (CPF) deductions, or SRS tax deductions.</li>
        </ul>

        <p>For an in-depth breakdown of work passes, Form IR21 tax clearance, and short-term 60-day exemptions, read our <a href="/blogs/singapore-tax-for-foreigners-expats-guide" class="text-link hover:underline">Singapore Tax Guide for Foreigners & Expats</a> and explore our <a href="/knowledge/singapore-tax-residency" class="text-link hover:underline">Singapore Tax Residency Guide</a>.</p>

        <h2 id="corporate-tax">17% Corporate Tax Rate & Startup Exemptions</h2>
        <p>Singapore maintains a flat corporate income tax rate of <strong>17%</strong>. Under the <em>Startup Tax Exemption (SOTE)</em> scheme, newly incorporated qualifying Singapore companies enjoy significant tax savings during their first three consecutive Years of Assessment (YA):</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>75% exemption</strong> on the first S$100,000 of normal chargeable income; and</li>
          <li><strong>50% exemption</strong> on the next S$100,000 of normal chargeable income.</li>
        </ul>

        <p>For all other active companies, the standard <em>Partial Tax Exemption (PTE)</em> provides a 75% exemption on the first S$10,000 and a 50% exemption on the next S$190,000 of chargeable income. Read our detailed <a href="/blogs/singapore-corporate-tax-guide-2026" class="text-link hover:underline">Singapore Corporate Tax Guide 2026</a> to learn more about Estimated Chargeable Income (ECI) filing and corporate tax rebates.</p>

        <h2 id="capital-gains-dividends">Capital Gains, Foreign Income & Dividend Rules</h2>
        <p>One of Singapore's key competitive advantages is the absence of capital gains taxation. Gains realized from selling shares, property, unit trusts, or foreign currencies are non-taxable, provided the taxpayer is not deemed to be trading in assets as a business.</p>

        <p>Furthermore, under Singapore's single-tier corporate tax framework, dividends paid by Singapore-resident companies are tax-exempt in the hands of shareholders. Foreign-sourced income remitted into Singapore by resident individuals is also exempt under Section 13(6) of the Income Tax Act. For full regulatory details, check our guide on <a href="/blogs/singapore-foreign-income-dividends-capital-gains-tax" class="text-link hover:underline">Singapore Foreign Income, Dividends & Capital Gains Tax</a>.</p>

        <h2 id="tax-reliefs">Personal Tax Reliefs & The S$80,000 Cap</h2>
        <p>Singapore taxpayers can lower their effective tax burden by taking advantage of statutory personal income tax reliefs. However, IRAS enforces an overall aggregate personal tax relief cap of <strong>S$80,000</strong> per Year of Assessment.</p>

        <p>Major tax reliefs include:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Earned Income Relief:</strong> Automatic relief up to S$1,000 (under 55 yrs), S$6,000 (55–59 yrs), and S$8,000 (60+ yrs).</li>
          <li><strong>CPF Cash Top-Up Relief:</strong> Up to S$8,000 for top-ups to your own Special/Retirement Account and up to S$8,000 for family members.</li>
          <li><strong>Supplementary Retirement Scheme (SRS):</strong> Contributions up to S$15,300 for Citizens/PRs and S$35,700 for foreigners are 100% tax-deductible.</li>
          <li><strong>Parent & Spouse Relief:</strong> Up to S$9,000 per dependent parent residing with you.</li>
          <li><strong>Course Fees Relief:</strong> Deduct up to S$5,600 annually for self-education and professional certifications.</li>
        </ul>

        <p>Learn how to maximize your tax savings legally with our comprehensive <a href="/blogs/singapore-tax-reliefs-deductions-rebates-guide" class="text-link hover:underline">Singapore Tax Reliefs & Deductions Guide</a>.</p>

        <h2 id="filing-deadlines">IRAS e-Filing & myTax Portal Deadlines</h2>
        <p>The Singapore tax year runs on a calendar year basis (January 1 to December 31). Key tax deadlines to remember:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>March 1:</strong> IRAS myTax Portal opens for e-filing.</li>
          <li><strong>April 15:</strong> Paper tax return filing deadline.</li>
          <li><strong>April 18:</strong> Online e-filing deadline via myTax Portal (Singpass login required).</li>
          <li><strong>May to September:</strong> IRAS issues Notice of Assessment (NOA) bills. Taxpayers must pay tax due within 30 days of the NOA date or arrange monthly GIRO installments.</li>
        </ul>

        <p>For step-by-step guidance on e-filing, AIS Form IR8A, GIRO setup, and credit card payment options, read our guide on <a href="/blogs/singapore-how-to-pay-file-income-tax-iras" class="text-link hover:underline">How to File & Pay Income Tax with IRAS</a>.</p>

